WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
All I wanted to do
was fix my email.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.100
I did not expect to have
a minor existential crisis

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.380
about how much the world
might be about to change.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.320
Normally this is the sort of talk
that I’d have given on stage

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.940
at some conference about
the future of the web,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.280
but, um, I’m in
New Zealand right now,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.520
and I also have this vague suspicion
that those conferences

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.040
might not be relevant
for too much longer.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.860
I was lucky enough to live through
the mass adoption of the internet.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.500
For anyone younger than
about 25 watching this,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.300
you won’t remember just how fast

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.580
and how incredible
that transformation was.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.140
And I was on the
cutting edge of it,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:34.860
I was living it,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.500
I was one of the people who
understood what was going on.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.940
Useful new technologies generally
follow a sigmoid curve.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.940
You have a slow takeoff as the
technology is invented

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.560
and the major pain points
get sorted out,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.260
and then you have this
incredible explosion of growth

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.460
as people find it useful

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.100
and the technology gets better
and better and better and better,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.140
and loads of competitors
enter the market,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.900
and people find more and more
and more and more

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.000
real-world, practical
uses for it.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.940
And there’s this massive race
to make newer, better, bigger things

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.640
that people keep making
more stuff with.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.760
And then you reach the limit of what’s
possible with that technology,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.680
and the rate of progress
flattens out again.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.320
Basically, there’s a reason
very few people

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.780
camp outside Apple Stores
for the new iPhone any more.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.740
I remember Napster from back in 1999.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.040
And in hindsight, I think Napster
was the first big sign

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.560
of just how many industries
were going to be changed

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.780
or completely destroyed
by the internet.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.040
Not just the music industry,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.400
but travel agents, video rental stores,
encyclopaedias, shopping malls,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.180
big-box stores, the postal service,
journalism, the entire media industry.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.320
Napster wasn’t the first sign,
but it was the first major one

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.620
that millions of non-technical
people were using by choice.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.920
The first one to get big mass-media
attention and legal problems.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.500
It was the first big
popular disruption,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.520
the first warning shot,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.920
the first rumble of thunder
off in the distance that said

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.380
the storm was approaching,
that everything was about to change.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.960
I don’t think many people realised
that at the time.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.240
And even if they did,
they couldn’t have predicted

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.760
the world that would follow.
The world I grew up in,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.920
the world that I’ve got
really comfortable with,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.980
the world of the web, of
social media, of smartphones.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.420
This video you’re watching now
is a very early-2023 video,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.980
and a very English-speaking one.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.860
This is a record of a point
in time and space.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.300
And I might be wrong about this,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.640
I don’t know where technology’s
going to go from here.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.120
But: I think that world,
my world, the one I grew up in,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.980
is about to change radically.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.600
I think we’re on a new sigmoid curve,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.020
and I have no idea how far along
that curve we are right now.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.420
And I don’t know if I want
to change with it…

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.980
but I think I’ll have to.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.280
And all I was trying to do
was fix my email.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.600
This is a really specific problem,
but it really annoyed me,

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.760
and I have to explain it for
the rest of this to make sense,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.480
so bear with me.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.300
Almost every email system
has the idea of folders.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.700
You have one copy of an email,
and once you’re done with it,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.200
you file it in a folder

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.060
like a piece of paper
in a filing cabinet,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.980
because that’s the mid-20th-century
physical analogy

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.120
that the inventors went with.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.380
But. In 2004,
Google launched Gmail,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.080
which threw all that out
in favour of labels.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.020
Email is just in one big pile,
you search to find stuff,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.640
and you add labels
to help you sort it.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.400
I hate labels, because
I like the analogy of folders,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.620
that’s what I started out with,
that’s what I was already using.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.600
I didn’t want to change for no reason.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.380
But everything else about Gmail
was so much better

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.300
than any of the alternatives
at the time

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.400
that I moved over to it
a couple of years later.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.540
And I decided, fine, I will
just treat labels like folders,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.340
I’ll change my workflow
as little as possible.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.260
Each thread gets one label.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.820
That’s like putting
it into a folder?

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.060
Which meant that when I backed up
my emails, which I do,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.340
never trust having only
one copy of something,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.140
particularly if that copy’s
up in the cloud,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.240
but when I did that,
I could use

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.580
a regular old-school
desktop email program

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.680
like Microsoft Outlook
to make that backup.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.840
You can use programs
like that with Gmail accounts,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.500
they talk to each other.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.080
If they didn’t, stuff like the
default Mail app on iPhones

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.120
wouldn’t work,
and people would complain.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.600
But those old-school systems
don’t understand labels,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.180
so Gmail just lies and
says they’re folders.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.220
Great! That means my
email program

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.740
can talk to Gmail, and request
a copy of all those messages,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.000
one “folder” at a time,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.340
and then I know it’s
backed up every message.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.160
They’re even stored in
actual email folders, like I wanted!

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.180
It took fifteen years
for me to notice:

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.320
that wasn’t working properly.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.940
I was doing a routine backup last month,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.160
and I noticed that a thread
in that email backup

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.140
was missing several
recent messages.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.700
And that made me suspicious,

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.340
the kind of discomfort that you get
when you know something is wrong,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.140
but you don’t know what it is yet.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.760
So I looked a little bit further,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.460
and I realised loads of messages
were missing in my backup.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.420
More than a hundred thousand
of them, over fifteen years.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.240
They were still there in Gmail,
on the web. They hadn’t been lost.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.060
It’s just they hadn’t been backed up.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.740
And that’s because, it turns out,

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.260
in Gmail, labels only attach to
individual messages

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.640
and not the full thread.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.380
That is invisible to most people.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.480
It’s barely mentioned
in the documentation.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.180
And it’s a really subtle,
annoying distinction.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.700
If you send an email,
put a label on that thread,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.800
get a reply, and then
just hit “Archive”:

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.540
that reply doesn’t get the label.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.640
And as you go back and forth,
and add to that thread,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.980
none of the other messages there
get a label

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.800
unless you explicitly
untag and retag the whole thread.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.800
Which means more than 100,000
unlabelled messages

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.180
weren’t in my backup.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.080
Is this a really specific, nerdy problem
that only I’m annoyed about?

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.300
Yes. Absolutely. I’m one of the very
few people in the world

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.200
that it’s going to affect.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.960
Loads of people don’t sort or label
their emails at all,

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.980
and most don’t back them up.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.680
But I needed to fix this,
I needed my backup to work,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.260
and I did not want to
change my damn workflow.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.800
But how on earth do I fix
every email thread over fifteen years,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.500
without painstakingly clicking
through all of them?

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.800
I grew up fixing things with code,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.540
so obviously,
that’s where my brain went.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.940
Google has a service
called Apps Script.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.980
It means that anyone
can write simple code

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.900
to easily change and
automate things

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.480
in Gmail, Calendar, Docs, YouTube,
whatever.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.140
It’s a brilliant service,
I’ve no idea how it ever got made!

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.020
And it will inevitably, one day,
be shut down with very little warning,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.640
because that’s just what Google does.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.420
But Apps Script meant
that to fix this problem,

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.640
I could just spend an hour or so
writing a bit of code.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.440
And it’s been a while
since I’ve done that!

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.160
First thing I do when I’m coding
something that’s simple like that,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.920
is I write out what it’s meant to do
in plain English.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.800
Just the flow of logic,
so I can then use that as a guide.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.540
So I wrote it,
and then I thought,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.780
hang on, isn’t there an AI thing
that’s meant to do this now?

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.180
Yes, this is a video
partly about ChatGPT,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.820
but I promise I’m not going
to go into the details there.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.200
Telling someone about your
fascinating AI conversation

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.860
is like telling someone
about your dreams.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.520
They don’t care, it just sounds
like you’re hallucinating nonsense.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.600
But I’d seen posts from people
being freaked out about

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.180
how good ChatGPT is and,
intellectually, yeah,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:40.740
I looked at it
and I agreed with them.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.060
It seemed surprisingly good.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.400
But it is just a text transformer.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.328
All it does is guess what the
next word in the phrase is.

00:06:47.328 --> 00:06:49.620
I knew that we’d been seeing
some improvements in that lately.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.060
I made a video two years ago about

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.420
“the sentences computers can’t
understand, but humans can”,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:55.800
and at the end of that video,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.520
I made a joke about how computers
with language skills are ten years away,

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.500
just as they have been
for the last 40 years.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:03.300
I was wrong.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.060
Turns out that’s pretty much solved now.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.720
But ChatGPT is still just a thing
that predicts

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.120
what the next word is going to be.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.100
It produces generic, milquetoast output

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.020
that’s confidently wrong
about a lot of things.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.580
It’s going to help spammers
and maybe have

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.320
a little bit of real-world use.
Right?

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.780
But I thought, okay,
let’s put my text description in,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.420
tell it to translate to this
obscure Google Apps Script,

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:25.260
see what it does.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.820
…and it wrote the code for me.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.900
In a few seconds.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.320
And I felt this pit-of-the-stomach,
existential horror,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.120
and at that moment,
I couldn’t explain why:

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.340
but I figured it out later.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.720
It’s just predicting
the next word.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.940
The code it gave back was
a bit wrong, but frankly,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.440
my first attempt would’ve
been a bit wrong.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:44.820
So I asked it in plain English

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.500
to fix a couple of the
errors that I’d spotted,

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.900
and it did, and that version
was nearly right.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.960
Not quite, though.
It used a really strange approach.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.180
I tried to work out
why it had done that,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.880
and then I realised,
I could just ask.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.760
And it told me, in English,
why it had done that.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.060
And it had made the same mistake
I did with my backup.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.340
Because, turns out,
Google’s documentation is wrong,

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.180
Google Apps Script itself treats labels
as attaching to threads not messages.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.600
Google wrote their own
programming language wrong!

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.560
So I told ChatGPT about the error.
And it fixed the script.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.020
It bodged its way around Google’s
bad documentation

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.240
to build the thing I was
actually asking for.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.220
And after all the
back-and-forths,

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.720
I didn’t actually
save much time!

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.400
But it was so much easier.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.380
I never had to
pore through documentation

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.620
to find the exact specific
magical incantation that I needed.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.400
Also, my coding skills are
years out of date.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.160
That’s one of the reasons I don’t
make computer science videos any more.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.960
The last time I learned a new thing
in code was probably 2015.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.420
My old skills were good enough.
I didn’t want to change my workflow.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.240
I’m not sure if ChatGPT is a
better coder than I am,

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.560
but it felt about equal.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.020
It felt like watching
while someone wrote code,

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.240
with me occasionally
chiming in and going,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.160
‘um, I think you might have
made a mistake there?’

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.800
And sometimes, I was the one
who made the mistake.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.940
At one point, I realised

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.880
I’d written the wrong thing in
that first text description,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.000
ChatGPT had just been following
my wrong instructions.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.780
I know it’s just a text model that
predicts what word comes next,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.080
but it didn’t feel like it.
So at first, I thought

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.060
that pit-of-the-stomach
existential-dread feeling

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.340
was because my brain
had gone ‘that’s a human’,

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.500
or ‘that’s an alien intelligence’.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.660
Or because I was
starting to think, well,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.360
what if my brain is just
a transformer system

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.220
that’s trying to predict
what the next word is?

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.980
I’m sure I remember some
old pop-science article

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.780
about how your brain’s
a prediction engine

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:35.760
and “surprise” is just a fancy word
for “being wrong about what comes next”.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.260
But then the next day I gave
ChatGPT a more difficult problem,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.780
one that required not just
translating English to code,

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.640
but actually a bit of
a logical leap,

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.200
and it just confidently, completely
failed in multiple, obvious ways.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.020
And that meant that I wasn’t worried
about it coming for my job.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.340
Not yet, anyway.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.760
Sure, no-one’s asked for that
exact problem to be solved before,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.620
the one about the labels and messages.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.840
ChatGPT wasn’t just copying
and pasting from somewhere,

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:02.820
there was arguably creativity involved.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.760
But, if I’m honest, a lot of the
programming I used to do

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.040
was just copying and pasting
bits from somewhere.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.900
At some point, every modern programmer
searched on Google or Stack Overflow

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.780
and “adapted” something
that someone else wrote.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.580
It’s basically part of
the job at this point.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.040
I’m deliberately avoiding
the argument about

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.260
creativity, AI art, and copyright,
by the way.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.820
I don’t know how
I feel about it,

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.460
and I don’t know how
the courts will resolve it.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.500
If I hired a human
artist or musician

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.560
to create a painting or song
in a more famous artist’s style,

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.500
that would be weird…
but it would be legal.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.900
As long as they’re not pretending
to be the famous artist,

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.560
as long as the smaller artist is not
trading on their name, that’s fine.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.600
The style of most of my videos
on this channel

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.780
is just a slightly faster version of
the TV shows I watched growing up.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.120
That’s what this intelligence was trained on.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.660
And this video is basically in the style
of every other video essayist on YouTube.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.240
It’s just I tend to go to the beach
a bit more often. [seagull screaming]

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.120
If someone set up an AI to
generate YouTube videos,

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.300
or even YouTube educational videos
as a genre,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:01.380
I think I’d be fine with that?

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.960
I don’t think I’d have a problem
with my work being a small part

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.060
of a massive pool
of training data.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:09.540
But if someone started ripping off
specifically my name and my work?

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:12.120
Yeah, I can see why artists
are furious about that.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.400
The British legal term for it
is “passing off”.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.360
I’ve said before:

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.160
please don’t train models
on what I make.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.800
Or, frankly, on anyone specific
without their consent?

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:22.560
But okay, I wasn’t worried
about being replaced,

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.420
and I wasn’t worried that
I was actually talking

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:27.180
to some human-equivalent
alien artificial intelligence.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.780
So why did I still have
that feeling of dread?

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:35.760
Artificial intelligence,
text transformers and diffusion models,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.140
everything that we’re
currently seeing,

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.140
seems to be on that
sigmoid curve of progress.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.140
And I don’t know what point
on that curve we’ve got to.

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.780
If we’re already most of the
way up that curve, then cool.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.120
Programmers and artists
have brand new tools,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.940
but they can’t create something
at a human level with them.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.380
It’s not going to
take many jobs.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.360
It’s gonna make people’s
work more efficient,

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.460
the same way that loads
of inventions,

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:57.960
like Photoshop,
have done before.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:01.800
If we’re at the middle of
that curve, then wow,

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.040
we’re gonna get some really
impressive new tools very soon,

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.500
that still need some
humans to work them.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.120
Maybe Siri and the Google Assistant

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.880
are going to become the things
hey were always promised to be.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.920
But that feeling of dread came
from the idea that ChatGPT,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.540
and the new AI art systems,

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:22.200
might be to my world what Napster
was to the late nineties.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.000
The herald, the first big warning

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.340
that this new technology, the thing that
was going to change everything,

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.400
was starting to actually
change everything.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.620
Where huge numbers of people,
not just the nerds,

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:32.880
were actively using it.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.220
And it didn’t matter that Napster
got sued out of existence,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.300
because by then there was Morpheus
and Grokster and LimeWire and KaZaA.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.980
And then there was Spotify.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:44.520
The old business model, that idea
that you bought a copy of music,

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:48.300
had been struck a mortal blow
and no-one noticed it for a while.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:52.740
If you hear echoes of the Napster case
in the lawsuits against AI art programs…

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.420
so do I.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.160
I was getting a haircut
the other week,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:59.760
and unprompted, the barber started
talking about ChatGPT,

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.160
and how he’d actually used it
to write a formal email

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.960
that he couldn’t be bothered
to put together himself.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.880
He wasn’t a particularly
technical person,

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:09.600
he wasn’t a nerd like me,
but he’d used it easily,

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.540
the same way that someone who wanted
to get a load of music for free

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.400
would figure out Napster.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.520
It’s not about ChatGPT,
not specifically.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.200
It’s about what it represents.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:23.220
Because if we’re still at the
start of the curve for AI,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:24.840
if we’re at the Napster point,

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.300
then everything is about to change,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.540
just as fast and just as strangely
as it did in the early 2000s,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:31.800
perhaps beyond all recognition…

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:33.720
and this time I’m
on the wrong end of it.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.460
I’m like the music executive,
back in ’99.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.240
It feels, to me, like something
might have just gone very wrong

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.340
for the now-comfortable world
that I grew up with

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.620
and that I’ve settled down in.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.480
That’s where the dread came from.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.120
The worry that suddenly
I don’t know what comes next.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:48.960
No-one does.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.300
I’ve been complaining for years
that it feels like

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:53.820
nothing has really changed
since smartphones came along…

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.100
and I think that maybe,
maybe,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.740
I should have been careful
what I wished for.

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:01.680
At some point, I’ll look back
on this video and, with hindsight,

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.500
I will easily be able to see
where we were on the curve.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:04.980
We all will.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.200
Part of me hopes that
I am entirely wrong,

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.180
that in a few years I’ll
still be working like this.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.100
The email thing is
a metaphor, of course,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:12.780
it won’t be email
folders for you.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.120
It might be something else,
something you’re attached to.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.800
Maybe something minor,

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.600
or maybe the whole
industry you work in.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:22.200
But right now, that feeling,
that creeping horror, that dread:

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.060
it turns out that was the worry

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.820
that after years of being
fairly steady and comfortable,

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.720
my world is about to change…
and despite everything,

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.380
I will still want my email
to be in folders.

